29:Schizophrenia is one of the most complex and heterogeneous mental disorders. The antipsychotic treatment is the first-line treatment for patients with schizophrenia. Many studies have suggested that the treatment significantly affects brain structure and function, yet individual differences in treatment responses are ubiquitous and prominent (Lin et al. 2021; Mehta et al. 2021; Matrone et al. 2022). Up to 30% of schizophrenia patients do not respond or respond poorly to standard treatment with antipsychotics. However, existing techniques usually operate at the group level to get an average profile of those who respond or do not respond to medical treatment, and provide no path to individual-level inference. To move away from group average statistics, neuroscientists and psychiatrists have adopted the normal growth charts approach, trying to quantify individual differences in brain and symptoms in patients suffering from mental disorders. As a promising approach, normative modeling posits that psychopathology characteristics of patients with the same diagnosis are not homogeneous, it highlights the value of understanding individual variation relative to group means (Marquand et al. 2016; Rutherford et al. 2022a, 2022b). Normative modeling has been applied in computational psychiatry, detecting individuals who deviate from the norm, including autism spectrum disorder (Bethlehem et al. 2020; Shan et al. 2022), major depressive disorders (Lin et al. 2023), Alzheimer's disease (Pinaya et al. 2021; Verdi et al. 2023), ADHD (Wolfers et al. 2020), and schizophrenia (Wolfers et al. 2018; Lv et al. 2020; Chien et al. 2022). Based on the normative model, this work aimed to examine how the deviations of the dynamic and static brain states are modulated by antipsychotic treatment, and whether the baseline severity of symptoms could predict the longitudinal changes.
31:Like the normal grow chart method, we must build a lifespan reference model to quantify individual variation against centiles of variation in a reference population to provide good lifespan coverage (Gur et al. 2014; Wolfers et al. 2020). Placing individuals into this coordinate system creates the opportunity to characterize their profiles of deviation. Recently, several studies tried establishing normative models charting brain structure lifespan trajectories and functional connectivity (FC; Rutherford et al. 2022a). Using 3 large data sets, Andre Marquand et al. (2016, 2019) established the normative model through different techniques and found that normative modeling features have the strongest statistically significant results when conducting group
37:difference statistics and classification tasks. Inspired by recent developments in mapping human brain charts, a recent study proposed that divergence between intra- and inter-person trajectory estimates varies with age and specific brain measures. They found that age-related brain changes inferred from crosssectionally mapped brain charts can substantially underestimate actual changes measured longitudinally (Di Biase et al. 2023). Motivated by this framework, psychiatrists have adopted the normative model in computational psychiatry (Oliveira-Saraiva and Ferreira 2023). In our previous study, deviations are quantified as having a z score with an absolute value larger than a threshold, and we found that compared with healthy controls (HCs), more patients with major depressive disorders showed deviation in the static and dynamic brain states (Lin et al. 2023), and we also identified a subtype of patients with larger extreme deviations from the normal range (Jing et al. 2023).
51:The Ethics Committee of the Peking University Sixth Hospital approved the study. Forty healthy subjects and 42 patients with schizophrenia were enrolled and gave written informed consent according to the latest version of the Declaration of Helsinki. Five patients discontinued participation shortly before the first session. For the following data analysis, 37 subjects with a mean age of 28.41 yr (SD = 8.41) were included (for demographic characteristics at baseline, please see Table 1). The symptom severity of all schizophrenia patients was evaluated using the PANSS before and after the treatment by the same psychiatrist. All the patients received mono- or dual-therapy with antipsychotic medications for 6 wk. The antipsychotics were selected according to the individual patients' symptom characteristics. The antipsychotic medications are listed in the Supplementary Material. All patients with schizophrenia underwent 2 experimental sessions. The first session began before beginning antipsychotic treatment. The second session occurred on the last day of 6 wk of antipsychotic treatment. Each healthy volunteer underwent one experimental session.
53:## Image data acquisition
55:The Cam-CAN data set provides detailed imaging data acquisition parameters, we also added this information to the Supplementary Materials. As for the data collected from patients with schizophrenia, the data acquisition parameters are presented below. Magnetic resonance imaging was performed using a Siemens Magnetom Trio 3.0 Tesla imaging system with a standard head coil at Peking University Third Hospital. The data collection details were as follows. For image registration, high-resolution structural T1 MRI data were acquired with the following acquisition parameters: 256 × 256 matrix size, 192 contiguous axial slices, 1 mm slice thickness, 1 × 1 × 1 mm 3 voxel resolution, 7 ◦ flip angle, 3.44 ms echo time, 2530 ms repetition time, and 1100 ms inversion time. Resting-state functional scans were obtained using a gradient-recalled echo-planar imaging sequence sensitive to blood oxygen-level-dependent contrast (2000 ms repetition time, 30 ms echo time, and 90 ◦ flip angle). The slice thickness was 4 mm (no gap) with a matrix size of 64 × 64 and a field of view of 220 × 220 mm 2 , resulting in a voxel size of 3.4 × 3.4 × 4.0 mm 3 . Each brain volume comprised 33 axial slices, and each functional run contained 240 image volumes.
93:## Image data preprocessing
99:For each individual, we computed dynamic FC matrices between all pairs of regions of the Human Brainnetome Atlas (Fan et al. 2016) using a sliding time window method (window length=60 s, step size = 1) (Chang and Glover 2010; Hutchison et al. 2013; Allen et al. 2014). Then, each FC matrix was decomposed into 17 networks based on Yeo-17 network template (Yeo et al. 2011). The average FC connection strength (aFCS, mean of FC strength from all window-wise FC matrices) and fluctuation characteristic of connection strength (fFCS, average change of the windowwise FC strength) for each Yeo-17 network was yielded from time-varying dynamic FC matrices. Finally, each individual's dynamic FC pattern was represented by 34 features.
103:Based on the large public data set Cam-CAN, we estimated sexspecific normative age models for each dynamic FC feature. We used quantile linear regression to estimate the normative range (Allen et al. 2014; Jing et al. 2023; Lin et al. 2023). To quantify the normative model, we estimated the 50th (median) percentile regression curves with bootstrapping confidence intervals. To avoid bias in the model, we computed the normative range by estimating the 5th and 95th percentiles in regression models. The gap between the 5th and 95th percentiles for a given age was as a scaling coefficient to normalize the feature deviation (Jing et al. 2023; Lin et al. 2023). To avoid the potential sex effects on model construction, we separately estimated the normative model in female/male groups. Furthermore, to improve the generalizability of the model, we estimated the normative models of healthy individuals from Cam-CAN under 10-fold cross-validation. Specifically, the data were randomly divided into 10 folds. Nine-folds were utilized for building the model, whereas the remaining fold was employed to evaluate the general performance of the model. This process was repeated 10 times, with each fold being excluded once. Finally, 10 models were aggregated into normative models for dynamic FC features.
111:dynamic (fFCS) FC, so 34 features represented each individual's dynamic FC pattern. Individualized deviation profiles of 68 features in SCZ patients (including before and after treatment, bSCZ:34, and aSCZ:34) were derived from sex-specific normative age models (separate models for female patients vs. male patients). For each patient, the features of the same age were positioned on the normative percentile curves from Cam-CAN to estimate individual deviation. We derived a Z -score with a scaling coefficient that quantified the deviation from the normative model for each feature as follows:
115:For each individual, index i represented individual's ID, and j represented one of 34 features. Yij was the observed value for the i th individual's j th feature. Y 50 ij , Y 95 ij , and Y 5 ij were the predictive features in 50th, 95th, and 5th percentiles for the same age, respectively. Thus, the z -score in formula presents the deviation of a certain individual's a certain feature and it is a statistical quantitative description of how much the patient alters from the healthy dynamic FC pattern. The average value of the 10 individual z -scores yielded from the aggregated normative models was the final features deviation for each patient.
119:General linear models and Pearson's correlations of the deviation profiles on bSCZ/aSCZ and clinical PANSS scores were conducted to explore the relationships between clinical symptom improvement and changes in dynamic FC patterns gaged with the deviation Z -scores. We used permutation tests ( n =5,000) to conduct the Pearson correlations to avoid false positive issues with sex, age, and head motion as covariates. The permutation correlation analysis is non-parametric, distribution-free, and particularly beneficial when dealing with a small sample size.
125:Table 1 shows the demographic and clinical characteristics of the study population. The data in the analysis consist of 37 adults with schizophrenia and 44 HCs. The data from the Cam-CAN data set were used to construct the sex-specific normative age model (as shown in Fig. 1C). And the comparison was made between the healthy comparison group and the patient's group. We also compared the distribution of the patients before and after 6-wk treatment.
131:Z -scores from 10-fold cross-validations, and a higher value indicated higher reliability of normative models. In this study, the average certainty for all participants from Cam-CAN was 0.991 for aFCS and 0.990 for fFCS, indicating that the normative models we built were reliable.
135:To directly compare the deviation changes after treatment, the heatmaps were created to show the deviation scores across 17 brain networks at the individual level (see Fig. 2A for aFCS and Fig. 2B for fFCS). The results suggested that the deviations in the brain networks' fluctuation significantly decreased after treatment. However, we failed to detect the pattern changes after treatment when analyzing the deviations of the averaged FC. Figure 2C shows the coefficients of variation (the ratio of the standard deviation to the absolute mean value) at network-level for bSCZ and aSCZ. Comparing the variability of aFCS after treatment with baseline, the coefficients of variation decreased after treatment for most of the brain networks, indicating that the networks of the patients converged to the mean state (the drug made them develop in one tendency). According to the changes in the deviations of the dynamic brain characteristics, we divided the patients into 2 groups: the normalization group, whose brain deviation networks decreased after treatment, and the deviated group, whose brain deviations became more distinct after treatment.Wefoundthatatbaseline,for fFCS,the normalization group has milder severity of the positive ( P =0.001, effect size = -1.21) and overall symptoms ( P =0.03, effect size = -0.76). These results suggested that the brain dynamic characteristics of those with more severe symptoms are more likely to be deviated from the normal range.
145:Fig. 1. Methodological overview.A) Dynamic FC matrices for each individual between all pairs of regions of the Human Brainnetome Atlas were calculated using a sliding time window method (window length=60 s). B) Each FC matrix was decomposed into 17 networks based on the Yeo-17 network template and the features for each Yeo-17 network were yielded from time-varying dynamic FC matrices. C) The sex-specific normative age models for each dynamic FC feature were built based on Cam-CAN, and patients' features of the same age were positioned on the normative models to estimate individual deviation Z -scores. D) General linear models and Pearson's correlations of the deviation profiles on SCZ patients and clinical PANSS scores were conducted to explore the relationships between clinical symptom improvement and changes in dynamic FC patterns gaged with the deviation Z -scores.
